A||||17||2M4 Dale||Sloppy Steering Column ???||||5||
Z||000000||2M4 Dale||05-19-2001||12:36 AM||||My 87 coupes steering column is very loose. Sloppy. Should I fix it or replace it ? I have a good spare column......Anyone ever tried fixing one of these things. I have the same problem in my 84 Chevy truck with tilt only I don't have a spare for it.||||reg||5
Z||000001||sjp777||05-19-2001||12:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2M4 Dale:<BR><B>My 87 coupes steering column is very loose. Sloppy. Should I fix it or replace it ?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It doesn't need to be replaced. The bolts inside the column that hold the tilt mechanism on have come loose. Happens all the time. The procedure to retighten them has been covered in this forum many times. Do a search.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||2M4 Dale||05-19-2001||08:25 AM||||I did a search.......according to Ogre I might as well swap in my good spare column. I don't have the tools to do this job.<P>Hey Ogre wanna repair my steering column ?||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||05-19-2001||11:02 AM||||It is a repair you can do for no parts required but it is very tricky. If you do it or pay to have it done, put in new cancel cam springs for turn signals.  If you pay to have it done it should only cost you around 2 1/5 hours. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||sjp777||05-19-2001||11:27 AM||||The only specialty tools you need are a steeering wheel puller and a lock plate remover. You should be able to rent both from any decent auto parts store for about $5<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||05-19-2001||12:26 PM||||Dale, it <I>can</I> be done but if you have a matching column from one of your parts cars it would just be easier to swap the column. I repair them here all the time but without the right tools it would be difficult.<P>sjp777, I hate to correct you but you need more than just the steeering wheel puller and a lock plate remover. You also need the pivot pin puller and an inverted 8mm torx socket to do the job right. There are four bolts that need to be tightened and it is impossible to get to the top two without removing the tilt mechanism.<P>------------------<BR>My Fiero Web Site.....<BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/index.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tgowens.gif">              </A><P>87 GT - 2.8L Gold automatic<BR>87 GT - 2.8L Blue automatic<BR>87 GT - 3.4L Blue 5-speed Getrag<BR>87 GT - 2.8L Med. Red Met. 5-speed Getrag<BR>88 Coupe - 2.5L Red automatic||||reg||1
Z||000006||sjp777||05-19-2001||12:53 PM||||I agree about the torx socket, but with a thin ratchet extension and swivel you can get 3 of the 4 bolts without pulling the mechanism. If you pull the bolts out and coat them with red loctite, that should be enough to hold for the life of the car. I've done it on 2 now and have never had it come loose again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||c m west||05-19-2001||01:14 PM||||there is no need for a "spare" column - just go in there and tighten the bolts - done deal||||reg||1
Z||000008||cadaver||05-19-2001||01:29 PM||||I didnt use a torx. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||05-19-2001||01:38 PM||||If you are going to fix it.... remove the bolts and locktight them.<P>If you have the time/tools/skill/etc then it can be done. When I do these I go thru and clean and grease everything. Replace what's worn, etc. Ending up with a pretty fully rebuilt column. <P>Cost wise, by the time you gather everyting up, it's usually cheaper to replace the column with a good used one. You can often find used ones that need nothing but the steering wheels switched.||||reg||1
Z||000010||cadaver||05-19-2001||03:04 PM||||But who is to say that a good used one wont loosen up in a few. I would rather just fix one and be done. Last one I did took like an hour. Then a buddy at work saw me doing it at lunch and conned me into doing the one in his blazer. Same column, same problem.||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||05-19-2001||04:40 PM||||I see you've been through that too Theogre!<P>Locktite, Locktite, Locktite and don't let it slam up when you pull the lever.||||reg||14
Z||000012||2M4 Dale||05-19-2001||09:41 PM||||HAHAHA......looks like I've started a good thread....lol Anyway, what I think I'll do is replace my column with a low milage spare and then attempt to repair the original. That does two things........fixes my car for now and gives me a little experiance and a fresh column for the future !!!!! Thanks guys !||||reg||7
Z||000013||Will||05-19-2001||11:21 PM||||I've had mixed results.<BR>Theoretically, if it takes 100K miles to loosen from the factory (which doesn't use loctite on them), why should it take less when Joe Schmo does it without loctite? I did it in my '86 SE, and it shows no signs of loosening again, after 7K or more miles. I did it in my '87 GT, and it is fully loose again after 5K miles.<P>I took the tilt column return spring out of both columns because I don't like it.<P>What needs to be replaced in a steering column?<P>There's no such animal as an 8mm torx. Internal T-35 (is that the right size?) yes, but no 8mm torx.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||sjp777||05-19-2001||11:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>I did it in my '87 GT, and it is fully loose again after 5K miles.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Stop pounding on the steering wheel in traffic frustration  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_1.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000015||maryjane||05-19-2001||11:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sjp777:<BR><B>I agree about the torx socket, but with a thin ratchet extension and swivel you can get 3 of the 4 bolts without pulling the mechanism. If you pull the bolts out and coat them with red loctite, that should be enough to hold for the life of the car. I've done it on 2 now and have never had it come loose again.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Had the same problem with my old cherokee(built by AMC) could only get to 3 out of 4 bolts. worked ok but I drive on a lot of caliche' roads and had to do it again a year later <P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||05-20-2001||12:58 PM||||Why Loctite?<P>just insurance. I did my own w/o it because it was night and I didn't have any. Normally I'd use it.<P>Again, As I've said of many other things.... don't assume the because GM did/didn't do something means it's the best way to go. The determining factor in what GM does is cost more often than not. GM likely figured theyed hold well enough to get past warrantee w/o it, so saved them selves the $.02 per column cost. When you figure this column was used in millions of GM cars/trucks... that's not small change.<P><BR>what wears.......<P>there's a bunch of the common items listed in my cave article. The biggest ones to watch are <P>the horn/turn signal parts. Anything cracked gets replaced. Other wise everything get's cleaned and lubed. (Be carefull what you lube horn contact/ring with. Not all grease works. If you have trouble leave it dry.)<P>the ignition rack gear. check the whole part, including the loop section for cracks.<P>Tilt Pivots. Inspect pivot area for cracks. If cracks are found, the column should be scrapped. Same if they are baddly worn.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||tgowens||05-20-2001||02:21 PM||||Actually Will, it <I>is</I> an 8mm - it is a Snap-On part #TLE80A and is referred to as an "E8". The Tilt Steering Pivot Pin Puller is Snap-On part #CJ134 (for anyone who needs it).<P>The point here is (I think), if you are going to do it you might as well do a complete job and do it the right way.<P>Dale, that's the way we do it here -- I have several already repaired and restored and it is easy to just replace the whole thing....||||reg||1
